<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Run settings of NumSharp.Tests, applied by `dotnet test` (and by Visual Studio and Rider) through the project's
  RunSettingsFilePath property. A settings file passed to `dotnet test` explicitly replaces this one; inline
  Name=Value arguments after the double dash are merged over it.

  The tests run in parallel, one test per worker (Utilities/TestParallelization.cs), and that makes VSTest's result
  reporting the bottleneck unless results are batched:

  BatchSize: how many results the test host collects before it sends them to the runner. The host keeps one cache of
  results and of the tests in progress, behind one lock, and flushes it (serializing every cached result AND every
  test still in progress to JSON and writing it to the runner's socket, with the lock held) as soon as
  results + tests in progress >= BatchSize. With the default of 10 and 32 tests in progress, that condition holds on
  every test start and every result, so every test serialized the whole in-progress list twice while the other
  workers waited on the lock: measured 2026-10-16 (i9-13900K, net10.0), a stack snapshot two seconds into the run
  found 16 of 32 workers blocked on that lock. At 1000 the host flushes once per ~1000 results, and each result is
  serialized once. Progress reaches the console once per batch, and the last partial batch at the end of the run.
-->
<RunSettings>
  <RunConfiguration>
    <BatchSize>1000</BatchSize>
  </RunConfiguration>
</RunSettings>
